Partial Answer 2: What kinds of people work at Google? What should be the role of HR in such a setting? What should be the role of managers? 

Google had a very data-driven hiring process that focused on ensuring employees had high levels of cognitive ability. Employees were self-described high achievers with a lot of skill in their area of expertise. Many employees described their teams with terms like hard-working, ambitious, and perfectionists. Because everyone was so skilled at their role, they valued collaboration and peer respect.

In this setting, HR acts more as a facilitator than a director. They move people as needed to support teams and changing business needs. It’s also important that HR develop top talent, ensure they have the resources they need, create fair rewards/benefits and prevent burnout. Google was known for very good compensation packages and perks, leading to very low turnover within the company.

